ntegers whose squares are two-digit numbers range from 4 to 9. We will now calculate the squares of these integers and check which of these squares end in 6.\n\n\\[\n4^2 = 16 \\quad (B=1)\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25 \\quad (\\text{ends in 5, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n6^2 = 36 \\quad (B=3)\n\\]\n\\[\n7^2 = 49 \\quad (\\text{ends in 9, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n8^2 = 64 \\quad (\\text{ends in 4, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n9^2 = 81 \\quad (\\text{ends in 1, not 6})\n\\]\n\nFrom the calculations, the squares that end in 6 are 16 and 36, corresponding to $B=1$ and $B=3$, respectively. Therefore, there are two distinct possible values for $B$.\n\nThe number of distinct possible values for $B$ is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Therefore, the integers whose squares are two-digit numbers range from 4 to 9. We will now calculate the squares of these integers and check which of these squares end in 6.\n\n\\[\n4^2 = 16 \\quad (B=1)\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25 \\quad (\\text{ends in 5, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n6^2 = 36 \\quad (B=3)\n\\]\n\\[\n7^2 = 49 \\quad (\\text{ends in 9, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n8^2 = 64 \\quad (\\text{ends in 4, not 6})\n\\]\n\\[\n9^2 = 81 \\quad (\\text{ends in 1, not 6})\n\\]\n\nFrom the calculations, the squares that end in 6 are 16 and 36, corresponding to $B=1$ and $B=3$, respectively. Therefore, there are two distinct possible values for $B$.\n\nThe number of distinct possible values for $B$ is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).","id":"number_theory:183"}
